  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Mainly dry but a few showers will be possible \nthrough tonight. \n\nMainly dry weather will continue ahead of a sharp cold front \napproaching the Lower Lakes today, although a few showers can't be \nruled out. It will be a bit warmer as southerly flow helps to nudge \ntemperatures up into the 60s for most locales. \n\nTonight...a potent shortwave tracking across James Bay will send a \nsharp cold front through the eastern Great Lakes. With the front \nexpect some additional showers to work through the region \novernight.\n \nKEY MESSAGE 2...Below average temperatures expected Monday, \nwidespread frost likely Monday night. \n\nA cooler airmass will settle in behind the front with 850 mb \ntemperatures dropping just below zero C (-1C to -3C) by Monday \nevening. Given the chilly airmass aloft moving across the warm lake \nwaters and some limited remaining shallow moisture it is likely we \nwill see some lake induced clouds and maybe even a few showers as \nthe trough axis pivots through the region The main story though will \nbe the early fall chill in the air on Monday, with afternoon highs \nonly expected in the upper 40s to 50s. It will also be quite breezy \nMonday, with winds gusting up to 35 mph in areas. \n\nSurface high pressure moving nearly overhead Monday night will \nallow temperatures to bottom out in the 30s, with some upper \n20s possible in the coldest inland locations. A widespread frost\nlooks likely during this period.\n\nHigh pressure will maintain dry weather through atleast midweek and \nwill also support a modest warming trend. Expected high temperatures \nto rebound to near normals with mid to upper 60s across the region \nby Wednesday.",
